hi my name is Ken Miller and I want to  talk to you today about why evolution  matters to biology not just biology but  all of science I got to start right up  front by telling you I'm not  evolutionary biologist I don't dig for  fossils I'm actually a cell biologist  and most of my career has been spent  working on the structure of biological  membranes especially the photosynthetic  membrane I use the electron microscope  that's where my training is I've used a  technique called freeze etching to look  inside those membranes and I've also  used image analysis and reconstruction  to develop things like a  three-dimensional model of a  photosynthetic membrane this ended up on  the cover of nature and more recently my  laboratory has also worked on the  translocon which is a little channel by  which proteins leave the ribosome and  enter the rough endoplasmic reticulum  that is a nice cell biologists get  interested in evolution there are  actually two answers to that  the first answer came from a student  when I first began to teach at Brown  University where I still am a student  came to me in the spring of 1981 and he  challenged me he asked me if I wanted to  debate a scientific creationist I had  actually never heard of scientific  creationism before and the more I looked  into it the more I thought yeah I might  like to go ahead and do this so the  students ended up establishing and  setting up a debate at my university to  my amazement an enormous crowd what  tickets to this event so many that we  had to put it in the largest room on our  campus which was the ice hockey rink  believe it or not and I participated  that year in another debate as well and  in fact in the two debates in that year  1981 more than 3,000 people showed up in  aggregate by given scientific talks  before that I'd never attracted a crowd  even close to that or seen that level of  interest that made it impression that  told me this was an important issue one  that people care about in the general  public and also I was appalled by the  amount of scientific distortion and  misinformation that was garnished in the  name of  scientific creationists a second thing  happened pretty much the same year and  that's when the debates was over but  when the debates were over a former  student of mine came to me with what I  thought was an outlandish idea and that  is how about you and I get together and  write a high school biology textbook  well after a few debates I decided that  was over I sat down with my friend Joel  Levine and Joe and I published a series  of biology textbooks designed for high  school students that have been very  successful and have been used at all  parts of the country that's the second  part of the story as to how I came to be  interested in evolution our books being  cutting edge biology had a very strong  treatment of the theory of evolution in  some school districts they were banned  in other school districts chapters were  cut out and in one very important school  district a warning label which you can  see right here was actually placed on  the surface of the textbook warning  students that evolution was the theory  and not a fact that brought me in a way  right back into the issue of whether  evolution should be taught as part of  mainstream biology now a lot of my  scientific colleagues asked me from time  to time didn't we settle all this in  1925 during the so-called Scopes Monkey  Trial and unfortunately I'm afraid that  what they're thinking of when they talk  about this is in fact the movie inherit  the wind which was loosely based on the  Scopes trial the reality is that when  William Jennings Bryan and Clarence  Darrow squared off in that Tennessee  courtroom John Scopes was convicted and  evolution basically disappeared from  science textbooks in the United States  for almost 50 years it's an  extraordinary thing and even today the  anti-evolution movement in this country  is thriving  Time magazine several years ago had a  cover story on that they called it the  evolution wars books pamphlets movies  and even a museum have been opened to  support the idea that evolution is  fundamentally wrong and the evolutionary  science is completely mistake  this sort of activity led to a dramatic  confrontation in 2005 in a federal  courtroom in the state of Pennsylvania  after a town Dover Pennsylvania decided  to order its teachers to prepare a  curriculum on an anti evolution idea  called intelligent design what followed  was a dramatic and widely publicized  seven week trial I had the honor but I'm  never sure that's the right word of  serving as the lead witness in that  trial and what you see right here is  actually the NBC TV courtroom sketch of  my cross-examination during the first  days of that trial at the end of the  seven weeks what happened well the judge  actually a conservative Republican  appointed by President George W Bush  looked at the evidence and testimony and  said intelligent design simply isn't  science in my opinion of course exactly  the right verdict and this was big news  it appeared on the nightly news on every  one of the major networks front page of  the New York Times everywhere you can  possibly imagine now despite this  verdict as it turns out or perhaps  because of the verdict a lot of us who  testified in the trial and I was just  one of several scientists who did that  ended up with some very interesting  requests when the trial was over I  appeared on a couple of TV programs  here's a snapshot of one of them and  those TV programs appeared unlikely  networks like Comedy Central and yes I  actually appeared as a guest on The  Colbert Report and if any of you  listening to this would like to find a  clip of that it's really easy to do you  just go to google and you type in my  name and Colbert Report and a couple of  appearances on Colbert will come up it  was an extraordinary thing it god I  think the message of science across to  an audience that normally wouldn't get  it but something interesting is the  puffing interesting comes out of that  and that's one of the key points that I  want to make all too many of my  colleagues in the biological science  community think that evolution is just a  story about the past and therefore  defending evolution is the job of  paleontologists and fossil hunters and  that sort of stuff but the reality of  science today is that evolution is  it's even in our blood and because it's  in our blood it's in our genome now what  I put up here is an image showing  hemoglobin the red proteins that carries  oxygen in our bloodstream we know the  exact location of the genes for alpha  and beta globin on a human chromosome  and there's something very different  different and very interesting about  their structure and organization we  actually have five copies of the gene  for beta globin we use these at  different times in our life cycle and  they were all produced any evolutionary  biologists would tell you by the process  of gene duplication but here's what's  really cool right in the middle of this  collection of genes and you can see it  right there is in fact a pseudo gene you  can see the Greek letter SIA that means  it's a gene that is broken now why do I  say it's broken it means that one of  these multiple copies accumulated so  many mistakes that it couldn't work  anymore what are the nature of the  mistakes you can see them here they  involve altered control regions  frameshift mutations even if this piece  of DNA could be transcribed it could  never be translate could never be made  into a protein now why is all of that  significant it's significant for a very  simple reason mistakes are unique they  occur only once and then they're  propagated to all of one's descendants  and that's what's happened with the beta  globin pseudo gene but the interesting  part is that we share not only the  structure of this beta globin locus but  we also share those mistakes with three  other organisms one know who they are  they're the gorilla the chimpanzee and  the orangutan and what does that mean it  means that all four of these species  these three guys in us share common  ancestry our genome is testament to that  but there's even more as a cell  biologist I've worked pretty much my  entire career on organelles within the  cells two of the most interesting are  chloroplasts and mitochondria and my lab  has done some research on both these are  extraordinary organelles that are  involved in the  construction of either chemical or solar  energy into a form that the cell can use  now there's some weird things about  these two organelles one of them is they  both import proteins from the cytoplasm  the rest of the cell and that's not  surprising but they import them take  them inside and then we export them into  their own membranes it's sort of a  roundabout route it's like going to one  side of the shopping center and then the  other and then coming back in and then  going back out again it makes very  little sense it also turns out they're  both surrounded by two membranes most  organelles in the cell only one how come  - it's a bit of a puzzle another thing  is they don't are they are not made by  the self from scratch they're  self-replicating in other words they  come only from the division of  preexisting mitochondria and  chloroplasts in addition the ribosomes  the protein synthesizing machines within  these are distinct they're very  different from ribosomes from the rest  of the cell why should these guys have  their own unique ribosomes and last but  not least they've got their own DNA they  have their own genetic systems why is  all of this the case well the answer  from evolution turns out to be very  clear and very straightforward  mitochondria there's very clear evidence  arose from primitive bacteria that were  then taken inside an early eukaryotic  cell and then surrounded by a second  membrane and eventually formed into the  organelles that today we call  mitochondria chloroplasts pretty much  the same thing except it was a  cyanobacterium a photosynthetic  prokaryotic organism and once you  understand this process which is called  evolution by endosymbiosis everything  makes sense protein import and re-export  the double membranes the self  replication of the unique ribosomes and  the fact that these organelles have  their own DNA it all fits together it  fits together quite beautifully today  evolution itself is a research tool we  use evolution to understand the  relationships of proteins in the  cytoskeleton for example we also use  evolution to understand the development  of body plants  in the field of evolutionary  developmental biology if you want to  read up on this pick up showing Carol's  great book endless forms most beautiful  and in this Carol explains the way in  which the animal body is modular it's  made up of repeating parts and these  repeating parts basically produce the  forms and patterns that we look at and  appreciate us biologists now one of the  problems that I see as a scientist and  an educator is that evolution acceptance  in this country is near the bottom of  the industrialized world there were 29  countries polled in which a larger  proportion of their citizens accepted  the theory of evolution then in the  United States we're at the bottom of  that list  the only country we beat out in Western  Europe was Turkey  why is that I think in large measure  that's because evolution is presented as  a fearful doctrine as something to be  afraid of and that fear that  characterizes or the induction of fear  that characterizes the anti-evolution  movement is dangerous not just because  it might cause people to reject  evolution but rather because it might  lead to us raising up a generation of  young people who have been taught that  science is to be feared and distrusted  and if we do that this country will give  up world scientific leadership something  that we simply cannot afford to do in  fact in the current year 2011  anti-evolution measures have actually  been introduced and attempted to be made  law in no fewer than seven state  legislatures I'd like to pretend this is  in the past but it is in fact an ongoing  problem so what do we make of this  defending evolution is really defending  the scientific method it isn't about  Darwin anymore it's about the science  that we do today and I think every  member of the scientific community  whether student postdoctoral researcher  faculty level research or independent  scientists owes it to themselves to  defend evolution for two reasons one is  it is the unifying principle that makes  sense of everything we do in biology and  number two acceptance of evolution means  acceptance and in  of science and the scientific method  nothing could be more important for the  scientific enterprise and in my opinion  nothing could be more important to our  country thanks for listening  you
 

